# Normalize line endings: store text as LF, and CHECK IT OUT AS LF.
#
# NOT `check out native`, which is what this line used to say, and the difference is a
# reproducibility defect rather than a preference. With core.autocrlf=true and no `eol=` on the
# default, every extension nobody thought to list is checked out with CRLF -- so a FRESH CLONE
# gets different bytes from the tree it was cloned from, and every test that compares bytes fails
# for everybody except the people who had never re-checked-out the file.
#
# Measured: a clean checkout of 9833e37 failed 108 of `internal/driver`'s golden cases, each one
# reporting a mismatch whose `got` and `want` looked IDENTICAL -- 17 bytes against 18. The
# testdata `expected.txt` files were the extension nobody listed.
#
# The lesson was already here, in the web-assets paragraph below, written when the same thing
# happened to Studio's page. It was written as a list of three extensions instead of as a rule,
# so the next extension inherited the old default. Rules generalise; lists wait.
#
# `text=auto` still DETECTS binary, so .png, .ico, .syso and the rest are unaffected: `eol=lf`
# applies only to what git has decided is text.
#
# Held by `internal/wiringcheck` TestTheCheckedOutLineEndingIsNotLeftToTheMachine.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The golden corpus, compared BYTE FOR BYTE by `internal/driver` TestGolden. Listed as well as
# covered by the default, because this is the extension that proved the default was wrong.
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.go    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.marco text eol=lf
*.mod   text eol=lf
*.sum   text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.ahk   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf

# The embedded web assets, which are READ BY TESTS and shipped inside a binary.
#
# `* text=auto` with core.autocrlf=true checks these out with CRLF, and Studio's page is
# asserted over byte by byte: `TestThePageBeginsAsADocument` reads the first line, and the
# page guards look for exact strings. A fresh clone therefore failed a test that passed for
# everyone who had never re-checked-out the file — found by walking this session's own
# commits to confirm each one builds.
#
# They are also go:embed'ed, so pinning LF keeps the asset byte-identical wherever it is
# built rather than varying with the checkout.
*.html  text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf

# Batch files keep CRLF, which is what cmd.exe has always been given.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Treat generated/binary artifacts as binary.
*.exe binary
*.png binary
*.tar binary
*.zip binary

# Vision benchmark corpus images.
#
# Full-resolution 1920x1080 game frames: ~3MB each, and the corpus is expected to grow as
# sequences are added. Ordinary Git would keep every revision of every frame forever, and a
# benchmark corpus is exactly the wrong thing to store that way — the frames are replaced
# wholesale when a sequence is recaptured, never diffed.
#
# The MANIFEST and GROUND TRUTH stay in ordinary Git: they are small, they are edited by hand,
# and their history is the interesting one.
fixtures/vision/v2/**/*.png filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
